## Access control model
`packages/auth/src/permissions.ts` defines the `statement` (resources → actions) and built-in roles:
- Resources/actions: `organization` (update, delete), `member` (create, update, delete), `invitation` (create, cancel), `ac` (create, read, update, delete), `project` (create, read, update, delete).
- Roles: `owner` (all), `admin` (all except `organization.delete`), `member` (`project.read`).
- `ac` is the resource that gates managing custom roles themselves — name it `ac` exactly; it is what the roles UI checks.
- The statement and the owner and admin grants start from better-auth's (`defaultStatements`, `ownerAc` and `adminAc` of `better-auth/plugins/organization/access`, without `team`, which is not enabled) and add `project`. Every action name is therefore one better-auth's endpoints check: `removeMember` needs `member.delete`, `updateMemberRole` `member.update`, `createInvitation` `invitation.create`, `cancelInvitation` `invitation.cancel`, the organization endpoints `organization.update` and `organization.delete`, the role endpoints `ac.create|read|update|delete`. Add custom resources next to `project`; never rename an action better-auth checks.

Custom roles are created at runtime via the `role` router (`auth.api.createOrgRole`) and stored in `organizationRoleTable`.

## The organization guard
better-auth lets a member whose role holds `member.update`, `member.delete`, `invitation.create`, `ac.update` or `ac.delete` act on any role, member or invitation of the organization, a stronger one than its own included. `packages/auth/src/organization-guard.ts` is a better-auth plugin in front of the `organization/*` endpoints, so a tRPC procedure, `auth.api.*` and a direct request to `/api/auth/organization/*` meet the same rules. It works on the organization the request names (`organizationId` of the body) and, when the body names none or an empty one, on the active organization of the session, as better-auth does; a request it cannot place in an organization the caller belongs to is refused before it reaches the endpoint. The rules live in `packages/auth/src/role-grants.ts`; a member holds a role when it holds every permission of it, and the owner holds them all.

### Giving a role
- A role is given as one id: lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, at most 40 characters, never a list (`isRoleId` in `permissions.ts`).
- The id is a built-in role or a row of `organizationRoleTable` of the organization; any other value, or a row whose permissions cannot be read, is refused with `No role has that id`.
- The caller holds every permission of that role (`canAssignRole`). A refusal is a 403. The `owner` role stays the owner's, as better-auth keeps it.
- `member.assignableRoles` (`packages/api/src/router/member.ts`) lists the ids the caller may give, and the members table and the invite dialog offer those and no others. The server is the guarantee, the list only keeps the interface from offering what it refuses.

### Changing or removing a member
`update-member-role` and `remove-member` also require the caller to hold every permission of the role the target member holds now (`holdsEveryPermissionOf`), so a role with `member.update` or `member.delete` cannot demote or remove an administrator. A member may still change its own role to one it holds and remove itself, and any member can leave with `organization/leave`.

### Role definitions
`create-role` takes a name that is one id (`isRoleId`; `owner`, `admin` and `member` are better-auth's). `update-role` and `delete-role` require the caller to hold every permission of the stored role, and better-auth already refuses a role definition that grants a permission its author does not hold. Without the first rule a holder of `ac.update` could empty a stronger role. A role is not renamed (see below).

### Pending invitations
The id of an invitation is what its recipient presents to join: `accept-invitation` checks that the signed-in account has the address of the invitation, and the project has open sign-up and no email verification (`requireEmailVerificationOnInvitation` is `false`), so anyone can create an account with an address they do not own. An id that another member could read would let that member join a second account with the role of the invitation. The plugin therefore decides who sees an id, for tRPC, `auth.api.*` and direct requests alike:
- `organization/list-invitations` and `organization/get-full-organization` return nothing to a member that can neither send nor cancel invitations (`invitation.create`, `invitation.cancel`).
- A member that can sees the invitations it could have sent: those whose role it holds every permission of (`canSeeInvitation`). An invitation whose role was deleted grants nothing and is seen only by a member that may cancel it, and one whose role is not one id is seen by no one. The owner sees every other one.
- Sending an invitation again (`resend`) returns the stored invitation, so it takes the right to see it and to give its role. An invitation to an address that already has a pending one cancels it (`cancelPendingInvitationsOnReInvite`), so it takes the right to cancel it and to see it.
- The recipient is not affected: `organization/get-invitation` and `accept-invitation` check the address of the invitation. Link delivery is how the id reaches the recipient, so plug a mailer into `deliverLink` before going live. The dashboard does not resend an invitation.

### Naming a role
better-auth accepts any text as the name of a role, and reads a name with a comma as several roles. A name is therefore one id, as above. The role form (`apps/web/src/components/roles/role-form-dialog.tsx`) asks `roleIdProblem` and words the answer with `roleIdMessage` as the name is typed, shows the error under the field and does not offer to create the role; the server refuses the same names for a direct request.

### Renaming a role
A role is stored on `member.role` and on `invitation.role` by its name, and `organization/update-role` renames the role row alone, so a renamed role would leave its members with a name no role has: they would hold nothing until someone noticed. The plugin refuses any `data.roleName` on that endpoint. To rename a role, create the new one, move the members to it, and delete the old one (better-auth refuses to delete a role that members hold). Editing the permissions of a held role is allowed and takes effect on the next request.

## Server-side enforcement
In `packages/api/src/middleware/rbac.ts`:
- `orgProcedure` requires `session.activeOrganizationId`, exposing it as `ctx.orgId`.
- `permissionProcedure(resource, action)` builds on `orgProcedure` and calls `auth.api.hasPermission`; it is typed on `Statement` (exported from `permissions.ts`), as are `usePermission`, `<Can>` and `RouteProps.permission`, so a resource or action the statement does not declare fails `tsc`.
- `assertInScope(row, ctx)` throws `NOT_FOUND` unless `row.organizationId === ctx.orgId`.

Routers use these — e.g. `permissionProcedure('project', 'create')` in `packages/api/src/router/project.ts`.

## UI gating
`<Can>` (`apps/web/src/components/can.tsx`) wraps the `usePermission` hook (`apps/web/src/hooks/use-permission.ts`), which calls `authClient.organization.hasPermission` (the endpoint `permissionProcedure` calls) through TanStack Query: one request per permission set, shared by every component that asks, never retried. Sidebar links carry a `permission` field in `apps/web/src/lib/constants.ts`. Every write action (new, edit, delete, invite, remove, cancel) sits in `<Can>` or reads `usePermission`, and a new one must too.

The query client (`apps/web/src/trpc/query-client.ts`) does not retry a 4xx answer in the browser (403 without the permission, 404 for an unknown id), only a server error or a dropped connection. A by-id page checks the row on the server (`projects/[id]/page.tsx` fetches `project.get` and calls `notFound()` on `NOT_FOUND`), so an unknown id answers 404 at once. The header shows the shared breadcrumb (`components/navigation/app-breadcrumb.tsx`), which labels a project by its name.

```tsx
{{{{raw}}}}
<Can permissions={{ project: ['create'] }}>
  <CreateProjectButton />
</Can>
{{{{/raw}}}}
```
